     "rot": 0,
     "text": "For the grown-ups: the numbers",
     "style": "heading"
    },
    {
     "id": "ffcb1c779",
     "kind": "text",
     "x": 0.6366666666666667,
     "y": 1.5866666666666667,
     "w": 7.226666666666667,
     "h": 6.276666666666666,
     "rot": 0,
     "text": "Pip’s dome is a real one. Every number in the pictures is worked out by the same code that designs the dome in The 40 Hour Cabin, from one measurement: the longest strut, 72 inches. From it come 19.42 feet across, 9.71 feet tall, 40 triangles and 120 wedges.\n\nThe frame weight, 2,478 pounds, is the wood alone: 65.22 cubic feet of green pine at 38 pounds a cubic foot, a published density. The screws and glue are priced in the model, not weighed. The 37.5 hours of frame work are the book’s own count.\n\nThe pictures are drawn in the book’s 3-D world, which is built from a heavier 15-inch log than the 12-inch log the numbers are for, so the frame reads at picture size. The shape is the same.\n\nDomes with the same longest strut take the same panels. On zeanahlab.com/domes, people with the same size dome find each other — just like Pip’s club.",
     "style": "end"
    }
   ]
